## Switch expressions (Java 14+)

```java
String category = switch (status) {
    case 200, 201, 204 -> "success";
    case 404           -> "not found";
    default            -> "other";
};
```

Several labels can share one arm, as in `case 200, 201, 204 ->`. With this switch a status of 404 gives `"not found"`, and only a value that matches no label reaches `default`. A switch over a `String` compares the selector with `equals`, so text read from a request matches its literal label.

- The **arrow form** never falls through: exactly one arm runs.
- A switch **expression** must be exhaustive: over an `enum` you can list every constant and omit `default`; adding a constant later makes the switch a compile error until you handle it.
- An arm that needs several statements uses a block and `yield`:

```java
int fee = switch (tier) {
    case STANDARD -> 499;
    case EXPRESS -> {
        int base = 999;
        yield orderCents >= 10_000 ? base / 2 : base;
    }
    case PICKUP -> 0;
};
```

## The old colon form falls through

```java
switch (n) {
    case 1: out.append("one ");
    case 2: out.append("two "); break;   // n == 1 prints "one two": no break after case 1
    default: out.append("other");
}
```

Prefer the arrow form in new code. Switching on a **null** String or enum throws `NullPointerException` (in Java 17), in the colon form and the arrow form alike, so check for null first.

Fall-through is why forgotten-`break` bugs exist: nothing warns you, and the next case's code simply runs. A case that ends in `return` or `throw` needs no `break`. The arrow form has been standard since Java 14, not a preview feature, and the comma list (`case 1, 2 ->`) states the one legitimate use of fall-through, shared code, directly.

## Pattern matching for instanceof (Java 16+)

```java
if (shape instanceof Circle c) return Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
if (obj instanceof String s && !s.isBlank()) use(s);
```

The pattern tests the type AND declares a typed variable, replacing the old test-then-cast pair. The variable is in scope only where the test is known to be true.

That scope follows the control flow. After `if (!(obj instanceof Integer n)) return;` the rest of the method runs only when the match succeeded, so `n` is usable there: `show(41)` prints `42`. The right side of `&&` can use the variable, but `obj instanceof String s || s.isEmpty()` does not compile, because `s` is not bound when the left side is false. A pattern never matches `null` (`null instanceof String s` is `false`), so no separate null check is needed in front of it.

## Sealed hierarchies (Java 17)

```java
sealed interface PaymentResult permits Approved, Declined, NeedsReview {}
record Approved(String authCode) implements PaymentResult {}
record Declined(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
record NeedsReview(String ticket) implements PaymentResult {}
```

`sealed` lists every allowed subtype, so the set of cases is closed and known to the compiler (pattern matching in `switch` over sealed types became standard in Java 21; in 17 you use instanceof chains or a method on each record).

Each permitted subtype must itself say `final`, `sealed` or `non-sealed`; leaving all three off is a compile error. A class that is not in the `permits` list and tries to implement the interface does not compile either. Records are implicitly final, which is why they fit sealed hierarchies so well, but sealing on its own says nothing about immutability.

## Loops and early exits

- Enhanced `for (var item : items)` for any `Iterable` or array.
- `break` leaves the loop, `continue` skips to the next iteration; a **label** (`outer:`) lets `break outer;` leave nested loops.
- `continue outer;` jumps to the next iteration of the outer loop instead of leaving it.
- Removing from a list inside a for-each over that same list is a classic crash: removing `"a"` from `[a, b, c]` that way throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. Use `list.removeIf(x -> ...)` or an explicit `Iterator` and its `remove`.
- **Guard clauses** keep handlers flat:

```java
if (order == null) return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
if (order.isShipped()) return ResponseEntity.status(409).build();
// main path, unindented
```

